# Core Engineering

Follow these rules for every change in this repository.

## Git Hygiene

- Keep commits atomic: one logical task per commit.
- Use Conventional Commits (`feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`, `chore:`).
- Never run `git push` unless the user explicitly asks for it.
- Never run destructive git commands unless the user explicitly approves them.

## Prefer Make Targets

- If a `Makefile` exists, prefer documented `make` targets over ad hoc shell commands.
- Use `make help` to discover available targets.
- Use `make check` for repo-level verification when it exists.
- Use `make docs-index` after adding, moving, renaming, or removing code symbols that should appear in indexes.

## Search Before Build

- Before creating a new function, module, component, hook, utility, or feature, search for an existing implementation.
- Check `docs/indexes/` first when it exists, then inspect the source files behind relevant entries.
- Extend existing code only when the semantics match and a shared API is clearer than a local solution.
- Do not generalize a component or helper just because the UI or code shape looks similar.

## Zero Dead Code

- A refactor is not complete until stale imports, unused variables, old state, removed components, and obsolete references are cleaned up.
- When deleting or moving files, search for all references across the repository.
- Prefer removing obsolete compatibility layers on unshipped branch work instead of stacking shims.

## YAGNI

- Implement only what the current business task needs.
- Prefer a simple function or local module over a new abstraction unless the abstraction removes real complexity.
- Avoid speculative extension points, unused configuration, and broad rewrites unrelated to the task.
